 Current source-bound ticket observations: `generators/3f26d45b9f51/evidence/supplementary-current.json`. Results are reviewer-reported; blocked or unknown stays explicit. These observations do not approve a generator or variant, resolve audits, or admit data to training.
 <!-- arcpuzzles-current-supplementary:end -->
+
+## Proposed episode-level `axis_mode` extension (2026-10-23)
+
+**Choice.** Keep production v4.0.0 and canonical rule hash `54129d31ff5b` unchanged. Add a separate, explicitly `proposed_unapproved` prototype under `workspace/parametric/` with one episode-level parameter: `both_axes` (default), `horizontal_only`, or `vertical_only`.
+
+**Reason.** This is a small lawful extension of the existing connect-dots family: marker colors, divider inference, coarse-cell geometry, and outermost-interval semantics stay fixed while the active connection axes vary. The default returns the canonical Racket program text byte-for-byte. Each setting is fixed across three demonstrations and four distinct queries, and every sampled input is required to produce three different Racket outputs, so the demonstrations identify the setting within the declared family before query labels are considered.
+
+**Public/private contract.** Public challenges contain only demonstration inputs/outputs and query inputs. IDs are SHA-256 hashes of the exact bytes written by `reviewer_support.public_challenge_bytes`; seeds, settings, Racket programs, provenance, and query answers remain private. A batch is fully prepared before any output directory is created, rejects duplicate visible payloads, and is materialized in ascending public-ID order. The ordering/preflight clauses were added after an adversarial review exposed that caller order was an insufficient export contract.
+
+**Evidence.** The task-local staged suite reports 35/35 passing. Seed-31 evidence contains three episodes, nine demonstrations, twelve queries, 4/4 canonical-default pairs matching, and no ambiguity among the three declared settings. A separate probe re-executed 21 labels in Racket, matched an independent Python axis mirror on all 21, checked 18 demonstration-rival and 24 query-rival comparisons, and found no hidden-field influence on public bytes.
+
+**Limit and next action.** This is not a production approval, universal identifiability proof, or training admission. The independent mirror shares the production lattice parser, and the finite analysis covers only these three settings and one retained seed. A maintainer can now review the compact prototype and evidence as a candidate variant without changing the approved generator.
+
